#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125286} :activity/id #uuid "3372fca1-80b5-5808-a2bd-c2a0e0659773" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049809 :topic/id #uuid "156d2779-eafb-5c80-bd7a-1ed2ab0b5ca6" :topic/math-academy-id 1937} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125286 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125730 :tutorial/content "We've already seen that if the function $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at the point $(a,b),$ then the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(a,b) = (x-a)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(a,b) + (y-b)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(a,b),\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nz = f(a,b) + (x-a)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(a,b) + (y-b)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(a,b).\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is differentiable at $(a,b),$ it can be approximated by its tangent plane at $(a,b)$ *provided that* $(x,y)$ is \"close\" to $(a,b).$ Thus, by replacing $z$ with $f(x,y)$ in the above equation, we get the approximation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + (x-a)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(a,b) + (y-b)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(a,b).\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the best linear approximation of the function $f(x,y)$ in an open neighborhood of $(a,b).$\n\nFinally, we can write the right-hand side of the above equation using the gradient vector $\\nabla f(a,b),$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx  f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b) \\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b\\rangle\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ac689477-f228-5dcd-9ffa-18559bfead85" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2414 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a9a58c4d-a5c9-575a-90d9-009acca5ba22" :step/math-academy-id 10795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124451 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124255 :question/id #uuid "37734955-23b7-52d1-881d-0c77f672c35a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404960}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "0c37b1f0-332e-5bea-b9b7-317063ab4734" :step/math-academy-id 10796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124608 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124420 :question/id #uuid "839eb8fd-39b8-5f1f-b833-7134d39cdf2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8674"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050293}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385906}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"} :step/id #uuid "f38bdda7-c2e9-534b-8cd8-d22757c051a6" :step/math-academy-id 32184 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125571}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124610 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124422 :question/id #uuid "54ac5be6-5b6a-58a3-a960-0d9f18e7fc88" :question/math-academy-id "e-8692"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383139}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"} :step/id #uuid "33df8c71-a53b-56e2-bd72-aceb78b19aa2" :step/math-academy-id 32300 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125287}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125852 :tutorial/content "So far, we've been using the fact that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at the point $(a,b),$ then\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe can extend this result to higher dimensions. \n\nFor example, if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at the point $(a,b, c),$ then \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle,\n$$\n\nfor all $(x,y,z)$ that lie in some open neighborhood of $(a,b,c).$\n\nMore generally, if a function $f(\\mathbf x)$ in the variable $\\mathbf x = (x_1, x_2. \\dots, x_n)$ is differentiable at the point $\\mathbf x_0\\in \\Bbb R^n,$ then \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) \\approx f(\\mathbf x_0) +  \\nabla f(\\mathbf x_0) \\cdot (\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0)\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf x$ that lie in some open neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "683c6bf8-6cc9-5f40-afe2-0857ca3340c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5547 :tutorial/title "Linearization of Multivariable Functions in General"} :step/id #uuid "45087c9a-e754-5204-87c1-5e7e8df40c40" :step/math-academy-id 34419 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125853 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x,y)=4-x^2-y^2$ at the point $P(1,1).$ We want to find the best linear approximation to this function, valid in a neighborhood of $P.$\n\nThe partial derivatives of $f$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-2x\\, & ⟹\\, &  & {f}_{x}(1,1)=-2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2y\\, & ⟹\\, &  & {f}_{y}(1,1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivatives are continuous functions at every point, which means that $f$ is differentiable everywhere, and\n\n$$\n\\nabla f (1,1) = \\langle -2, -2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best linear approximation of $f$ in a neighborhood of $(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \\approx f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+(-2)\\cdot (x-1)+(-2)\\cdot (y-1) \\\\  & =2-2x+2-2y+2 \\\\  & =6-2x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the diagram below. Notice that in the neighborhood of $(x,y)=(1,1)$, the surface and its tangent plane approximation are very close.\n\n![](images/20/20f06226ec7c21583e56249ea2fe5c65c6127cef8a6e05ee4b1d7521ce49dc38.png)\n\nTherefore, we can use this to approximate values of $f(x,y)$ that are close to $(1,1).$ For example, using our approximation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,1.01) & \\approx 6-2(1.01)-2(1.01) \\\\  & =1.96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe actual value of $f$ at this point is $1.9598,$ so our approximation is pretty good!" :tutorial/id #uuid "eb2571f8-ee0b-571d-bacf-34a1e469da8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5593 :tutorial/title "Linearizing a Quadratic Function"} :step/id #uuid "75f15315-a63f-503e-9c27-079061856ae0" :step/math-academy-id 34498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125554}}] :activity/title "Linearization of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124729}]}
